gorilla-portable-network-hub (@VERSION@) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The roster shows the name the kid picked, not the phone model. Picking
    a name was the point; the screen forgot to listen.
  * READ opens files in a viewer with a BACK TO THE FILES bar. A bare file
    inside a sign-in sheet, which has no back button, was a room with no
    door: "I am forever stuck on that page viewing it."
  * The class page is served with no-store. Swiping back onto a cached
    copy carried already-spent one-time tokens, so sending quietly did
    nothing and every button looked dead until a manual refresh.
  * A plain-words hint under hand-in for sign-in sheets that disable the
    file chooser: open the normal browser and use the address instead.
  * Tab completion in the folder fields, directories only: a unique
    prefix completes with its slash, an ambiguous one stops at the shared
    part and shows the choices. Decades of fingers know what Tab does.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  @DATE@

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Say so, loudly, when another program owns port 80 while the hotspot is
    up: the sign-in screen would bring every joining phone to THAT program
    instead of the lesson. This happened in testing (a leftover test copy
    hijacked a real phone's sign-in) and the teacher's screen said nothing.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 09:10:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every device is asked for a name before the page opens up, once per
    lesson. Notes and handed-in work then carry the name a teacher can
    actually mark: "Amina--essay.docx", "Johnny: I do not understand".
    The permanent notes record keeps name, device model AND address side
    by side, so thirty identical messages sent for kicks are thirty
    attributable lines.
  * Downloads now declare their real type. They were served as "unknown
    binary" to force saving, and Android believed it: tapping a finished
    download said "We can't open this file". Found on a real phone.
  * Friendly names on the hotspot: typing "classroom/" (or class, lesson,
    school, hub, each with the slash) opens the page. No colons, no
    addresses; a colon is three keyboard layers deep on a phone.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 09:05:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=high

  * Fix: the wifi-restore safety fuse fired INTO the running lesson every
    three to four minutes, because a systemd timer's deadline cannot be
    pushed back by re-arming under the same name. The fuse is now one
    transient service holding a sleep, and the heartbeat restarts it, which
    truly moves the deadline. Proven with a short fuse before shipping:
    beats push it back, abandonment fires it, stopping disarms it.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 08:35:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The class page: a phone that joins the wifi gets its own sign-in screen
    popped by the operating system, and the sign-in screen is the lesson.
    Big buttons, works with no JavaScript on browsers back to 2009.
  * Hand-in: kids send work back from the same page. Retries land once,
    a changed resubmission is kept as a second version, and nothing in
    handed-in/ is ever served back to the class.
  * Notes: a kid can send a short note to the teacher; the teacher can put
    a notice at the top of every kid's page.
  * The tick list: the teacher chooses file by file what the network can
    see, before starting and live during the lesson.
  * Serves on port 80 as well as 8080 (the install grants that one
    capability), and answers the connectivity probes of Android, iOS,
    Windows, GNOME, KDE and Firefox via a NetworkManager dnsmasq drop-in.
  * USB drives: hand-in switches itself off loudly on a read-only drive,
    uploads are synced before they get their final name, and the roster
    says so if the folder vanishes mid-lesson.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 08:20:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First package. One binary with a screen and four commands: serve, get,
    sums and doctor.
  * Running "hub" with no arguments opens the screen rather than printing
    usage, which is what a double-click passes.
  * Creates a WPA2 wifi network through NetworkManager, never an open one,
    and restores the previous connection through a systemd timer that
    survives the program being killed.
  * Finds the machine handing out files by asking the gateway first, then
    sweeping the local /24.
  * Byte ranges and keep-alive on both sides, so an interrupted transfer
    carries on instead of starting again.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Sun, 24 Aug 2026 23:20:00 +0100
